WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.281 --> 00:00:01.114
- Hello.

00:00:01.114 --> 00:00:04.587
My name is Sammi, and I am
from Paris, France originally.

00:00:04.587 --> 00:00:06.508
From Atlanta, Georgia.

00:00:06.508 --> 00:00:08.003
From Sydney, Australia.

00:00:08.003 --> 00:00:08.902
London, England.

00:00:08.902 --> 00:00:10.105
Moscow, Russia.

00:00:10.105 --> 00:00:11.210
There's been a lot more,

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.062
but those are the ones that come to mind.

00:00:12.062 --> 00:00:14.695
My name is Sammi Grant,
I'm a dialect coach

00:00:14.695 --> 00:00:17.079
and I'm legally blind.

00:00:17.079 --> 00:00:20.579
(people speaking at once)

00:00:21.586 --> 00:00:26.203
A dialect coach is someone
who teaches actors accents.

00:00:26.203 --> 00:00:28.642
I can do accents from around the world.

00:00:28.642 --> 00:00:30.191
I don't really even have a count anymore

00:00:30.191 --> 00:00:32.078
because I've taught so many.

00:00:32.078 --> 00:00:34.310
I mostly work for the stage.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:37.036
I've started dabbling in television.

00:00:37.036 --> 00:00:39.797
One of the reasons I think
that I took to voice work

00:00:39.797 --> 00:00:44.269
and dialect work is because
I'm more in tune with hearing.

00:00:44.269 --> 00:00:46.111
So people are always like, “Oh,
you have heightened hearing.”

00:00:46.111 --> 00:00:47.446
I don't like that phrase

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:49.813
'cause it sounds like we
have like superhero hearing

00:00:49.813 --> 00:00:51.765
like we're Daredevil or something.

00:00:51.765 --> 00:00:52.981
Oh, you're just like Daredevil.

00:00:52.981 --> 00:00:53.853
No.

00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:55.218
But I do have more a tuned hearing.

00:00:55.218 --> 00:00:57.606
I use my hearing more
than a sighted person

00:00:57.606 --> 00:00:59.672
because I have to.

00:00:59.672 --> 00:01:02.649
For a London accent,
placement's really important

00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:06.007
so the placements like
really forwards in the mouth.

00:01:06.007 --> 00:01:10.460
So there's the southern
accent that lacks the R.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:11.783
The Australian accent quite different

00:01:11.783 --> 00:01:12.741
than the British accent

00:01:12.741 --> 00:01:14.740
so they don't really say like, buttah,

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.329
it's a little more like butter.

00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:17.836
Transatlantic accent is very dramatic.

00:01:17.836 --> 00:01:19.635
It's what you hear in
those old time movies.

00:01:19.635 --> 00:01:21.835
It's kind of like a,
well, well I don't know,

00:01:21.835 --> 00:01:22.809
is he ever gonna love me?

00:01:22.809 --> 00:01:23.959
Well I just want him to love me.

00:01:23.959 --> 00:01:26.714
But if I'm too expressive
maybe he won't, I don't know.

00:01:26.714 --> 00:01:28.231
For Russian accent you
want to sort of feel like

00:01:28.231 --> 00:01:30.388
you swallow some in back of the mouth.

00:01:30.388 --> 00:01:31.555
This is very stereotype,

00:01:31.555 --> 00:01:33.407
but like the Jewish New York mother.

00:01:33.407 --> 00:01:35.235
This is what I do a lot with my friends.

00:01:35.235 --> 00:01:36.838
You know, kind of like
the, why don't you call me?

00:01:36.838 --> 00:01:38.987
You know I sit around and
wait for your calls all day

00:01:38.987 --> 00:01:39.820
and you never call me.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.848
I don't mind, it's okay,
you know, just whatever.

00:01:41.848 --> 00:01:43.940
I just pay for your
college tuition, it's okay.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.626
It's a pretty in depth process
teaching someone an accent.

00:01:46.626 --> 00:01:50.599
I get sent this script and I
try to gather everything I can.

00:01:50.599 --> 00:01:53.842
So that goes much beyond just,
where does this take place?

00:01:53.842 --> 00:01:56.535
It also factors in time period

00:01:56.535 --> 00:01:59.277
and education level, class level,

00:01:59.277 --> 00:02:01.972
and I also think about the
gender of my character.

00:02:01.972 --> 00:02:04.305
And then I talk with the
director to sort of you know

00:02:04.305 --> 00:02:08.300
touch base on their concept
for the voice of the show.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.073
And then I kind of go on a hunt

00:02:11.073 --> 00:02:13.545
for authentic speakers.

00:02:13.545 --> 00:02:15.348
Once I've gathered all that information

00:02:15.348 --> 00:02:17.515
I present it to the actor.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.737
We're now surrounded by so many voices

00:02:20.737 --> 00:02:21.982
that are emotionless.

00:02:21.982 --> 00:02:24.713
You know by these automated
voices that sound very fake.

00:02:24.713 --> 00:02:26.545
Where it's like, I'd love to help you,

00:02:26.545 --> 00:02:28.213
I'm a computer that's here to help you

00:02:28.213 --> 00:02:31.085
with whatever you want all the time.

00:02:31.085 --> 00:02:33.418
I love meeting new people
and hearing their voices.

00:02:33.418 --> 00:02:36.292
I, you know, immediately
go into dialect coach mode

00:02:36.292 --> 00:02:37.667
and I wanna sort of imitate them.

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:40.931
I just wanna like experience their voice

00:02:40.931 --> 00:02:43.266
and experience what their vocalizing.

00:02:43.266 --> 00:02:46.766
(people speaking at once)

